WEBVTT

00:00.434 --> 00:01.850
What I want to do
in this video is

00:01.850 --> 00:04.690
think about the
origins of algebra.

00:04.690 --> 00:08.390
The origins of
algebra, and the word,

00:08.390 --> 00:10.640
especially in association
with the ideas

00:10.640 --> 00:15.617
that algebra now represents,
comes from this book,

00:15.617 --> 00:17.950
or actually this is a page
of the book right over there.

00:17.950 --> 00:20.430
The English translation
for the title of this book

00:20.430 --> 00:23.050
is the "Compendious
Book on Calculation

00:23.050 --> 00:25.280
by Completion and Balancing."

00:25.280 --> 00:29.120
And it was written by a
Persian mathematician who

00:29.120 --> 00:32.052
lived in Baghdad
in, I believe, it

00:32.052 --> 00:33.510
was in the eighth
or ninth century.

00:33.510 --> 00:37.740
I believe it was actually 820
AD when he wrote this book.

00:37.740 --> 00:38.620
AD.

00:38.620 --> 00:42.730
And algebra is the Arabic word,
that here is the actual title

00:42.730 --> 00:45.070
that he gave to it, which
is the Arabic title.

00:45.070 --> 00:47.456
Algebra means restoration
or completion.

00:50.600 --> 00:55.110
Restoration or completion.

00:55.110 --> 00:58.380
And he associated it in his book
with a very specific operation,

00:58.380 --> 01:01.490
really taking something
from one side of an equation

01:01.490 --> 01:03.659
to another side of an equation.

01:03.659 --> 01:05.450
But we can actually
see it right over here,

01:05.450 --> 01:07.075
and I don't know
Arabic, but I actually

01:07.075 --> 01:08.550
do know some
languages that seems

01:08.550 --> 01:10.330
to have borrowed a
little bit from Arabic,

01:10.330 --> 01:12.310
or maybe it went the
other way around.

01:12.310 --> 01:15.770
But this says Al-kitab,
and I know just enough

01:15.770 --> 01:18.350
Urdu and Hindi to understand
a good India movie,

01:18.350 --> 01:20.170
but Al-kitab, kitab means book.

01:20.170 --> 01:22.550
So this part is book.

01:22.550 --> 01:23.170
Book.

01:23.170 --> 01:26.816
Al-mukhtasar, well, I think
that means compendious,

01:26.816 --> 01:28.690
because I don't know
the word for compendious

01:28.690 --> 01:29.930
and that seems like that.

01:29.930 --> 01:33.170
Fihisab, hisab means
calculation in Hindi or Urdu,

01:33.170 --> 01:35.210
so this is calculation.

01:35.210 --> 01:36.300
Calculation.

01:36.300 --> 01:38.500
Al-gabr, this is the root.

01:38.500 --> 01:41.110
This is the famous algebra,
this is where it shows up.

01:41.110 --> 01:45.520
So this is for completion, you
could view that as completion.

01:45.520 --> 01:46.450
Completion.

01:46.450 --> 01:52.050
And then wa'l-muqabala, and that
means essentially balancing.

01:52.050 --> 01:52.660
Balancing.

01:52.660 --> 01:55.305
Completion and balancing.

01:55.305 --> 01:56.680
So if we wanted
to translate it--

01:56.680 --> 01:58.890
I know this isn't a video
on translating Arabic,

01:58.890 --> 02:04.100
but the book, I guess
this is saying compendious

02:04.100 --> 02:10.720
on calculation by
completion and balancing

02:10.720 --> 02:12.580
is the rough translation
right over there.

02:12.580 --> 02:15.470
But that is the source
of the word algebra,

02:15.470 --> 02:17.940
and this is a very, very,
very important book.

02:17.940 --> 02:20.700
Not just because it was the
first use of the word algebra,

02:20.700 --> 02:24.720
but many people viewed
this book as the first time

02:24.720 --> 02:29.800
that algebra took a
lot of its modern--

02:29.800 --> 02:31.940
took on many of
its modern ideas.

02:31.940 --> 02:34.240
Ideas of balancing an equation.

02:34.240 --> 02:36.320
The abstract problem
itself, not trying

02:36.320 --> 02:38.540
to do one off problems
here or there.

02:38.540 --> 02:42.160
But al-Khwarizmi was
not the first person,

02:42.160 --> 02:44.640
and just to get an idea of
where all this is happening.

02:44.640 --> 02:47.122
So he was hanging
out in Baghdad,

02:47.122 --> 02:48.580
and this part of
the world shows up

02:48.580 --> 02:50.130
a lot in the history of algebra.

02:50.130 --> 02:51.650
But he was hanging
out right there

02:51.650 --> 02:54.050
in around the eighth
or ninth century.

02:54.050 --> 02:56.150
So let me draw a
time line here, just

02:56.150 --> 02:58.230
so we can appreciate everything.

02:58.230 --> 03:03.590
So that is timeline,
and then whether or not

03:03.590 --> 03:06.710
you are religious, most
of our modern dates

03:06.710 --> 03:11.720
are dependent on the birth of
Jesus, so that is right there.

03:11.720 --> 03:13.905
Maybe I'll put a cross
over there to signify that.

03:13.905 --> 03:15.280
When we want to
be non-religious,

03:15.280 --> 03:17.110
we say the common era.

03:17.110 --> 03:19.750
Before the common era, when
we want to be religious

03:19.750 --> 03:22.530
we say AD, which means
in the year of our lord.

03:22.530 --> 03:25.120
I don't know the Latin,
Anno Domini, I believe,

03:25.120 --> 03:26.220
year of our lord.

03:26.220 --> 03:28.970
And then when we want--
in the religious context,

03:28.970 --> 03:32.180
instead of saying before common
era, we say before Christ, BC.

03:32.180 --> 03:36.790
But either way, so this
is 1000 in the common era.

03:36.790 --> 03:39.656
This is 2000 in the common era.

03:39.656 --> 03:41.530
And obviously, we are
sitting-- at least when

03:41.530 --> 03:44.400
I'm making this video, I'm
sitting right about there.

03:44.400 --> 03:48.560
And then this is 1000
before the common era,

03:48.560 --> 03:52.210
and this is 2000
before the common era.

03:52.210 --> 03:54.565
So the first traces--
and I'm skipping out,

03:54.565 --> 03:56.190
and really, it's just
what we can find.

03:56.190 --> 03:57.420
I'm sure if we were
able to dig more,

03:57.420 --> 03:59.044
we might be able to
find other evidence

03:59.044 --> 04:02.080
of different civilizations
and different people stumbling

04:02.080 --> 04:04.720
on many of the ideas in algebra.

04:04.720 --> 04:07.430
But our first records of
people really exploring

04:07.430 --> 04:09.690
the ideas that are
hit upon in algebra

04:09.690 --> 04:13.590
come from ancient
Babylon around 2000 years

04:13.590 --> 04:16.160
before the common
era, before Christ.

04:16.160 --> 04:22.562
So right around there
there are stone tablets

04:22.562 --> 04:24.270
where it looks like
people were exploring

04:24.270 --> 04:26.080
some of the fundamental
ideas of algebra.

04:26.080 --> 04:27.880
They weren't using
the same symbols.

04:27.880 --> 04:30.780
They weren't using the same ways
of representing the numbers,

04:30.780 --> 04:33.090
but it was algebra that
they were working on.

04:33.090 --> 04:35.430
And that was, once again,
in this part of the world.

04:35.430 --> 04:38.760
Babylon was right about there.

04:38.760 --> 04:42.720
And Babylon, it's kind of
kept the tradition of Sumeria.

04:42.720 --> 04:44.920
This whole region was
called Mesopotamia,

04:44.920 --> 04:47.290
Greek for between two rivers.

04:47.290 --> 04:49.230
But that's the first
traces of people

04:49.230 --> 04:51.270
that we know of that
where people were starting

04:51.270 --> 04:54.550
to do what we would
call real, real algebra.

04:54.550 --> 04:55.892
And then you fast forward.

04:55.892 --> 04:57.600
And I'm sure we're
missing-- and I'm sure

04:57.600 --> 05:01.960
even our historians don't know
all of the different instances

05:01.960 --> 05:08.300
of people using algebra, but the
major contributions to algebra,

05:08.300 --> 05:11.450
we saw it here in
Babylon 2000 years ago.

05:11.450 --> 05:13.990
And then if we fast
forward to about 200 to 300

05:13.990 --> 05:17.310
AD, so right over there,
you have a Greek gentleman

05:17.310 --> 05:18.870
who lived in Alexandria.

05:18.870 --> 05:21.020
So this is Greece
right over here,

05:21.020 --> 05:23.210
but he lived in Alexandria,
which at the time

05:23.210 --> 05:25.240
was part of the Roman Empire.

05:25.240 --> 05:27.890
So Alexandria is
right over here,

05:27.890 --> 05:30.630
and he was a gentleman by
the name of Diophantus,

05:30.630 --> 05:31.890
or Diophantus.

05:31.890 --> 05:36.300
I don't know how to
pronounce it, Diophantus.

05:36.300 --> 05:38.550
And he is sometimes
credited with being

05:38.550 --> 05:41.330
the father of algebra,
and it's debatable

05:41.330 --> 05:44.200
whether it's Diophantus
or al-Khwarizmi.

05:44.200 --> 05:45.770
al-Khwarizmi, who
kind of started

05:45.770 --> 05:48.320
using these terms of
balancing equations

05:48.320 --> 05:50.050
and talking about
math in a purer way,

05:50.050 --> 05:53.370
while Diophantus was more
focused on particular problems.

05:53.370 --> 05:55.840
And both of them were
kind of beat to the punch

05:55.840 --> 05:57.780
by the Babylonians,
although they all

05:57.780 --> 05:59.170
did contribute in their own way.

05:59.170 --> 06:00.140
It's not like they
were just copying

06:00.140 --> 06:01.370
what the Babylonians did.

06:01.370 --> 06:03.230
They had their own
unique contributions

06:03.230 --> 06:06.360
to what we now consider algebra.

06:06.360 --> 06:08.170
But many, especially
Western historians,

06:08.170 --> 06:11.480
associate Diophantus as
the father of algebra.

06:11.480 --> 06:13.580
And now, al-Khwarizmi
is sometimes

06:13.580 --> 06:16.390
what other people would argue
as the father of algebra,

06:16.390 --> 06:18.290
so he made significant
contributions.

06:18.290 --> 06:22.200
And if you go to 600 AD-- so
if you go to about 600 AD,

06:22.200 --> 06:26.140
another famous mathematician
in the history of algebra

06:26.140 --> 06:29.200
was Brahmagupta, in India.

06:29.200 --> 06:32.580
Brahmagupta, in India.

06:32.580 --> 06:34.180
So obviously, and
actually, I don't

06:34.180 --> 06:35.490
know where in India he lived.

06:35.490 --> 06:37.900
I should look that
up, but roughly

06:37.900 --> 06:39.440
in that part of the world.

06:39.440 --> 06:41.665
And he also made
significant contributions.

06:44.290 --> 06:45.790
And then you have
al-Khwarizmi, who

06:45.790 --> 06:50.530
shows up right
there, al-Khwarizmi.

06:50.530 --> 06:53.560
And he is the gentleman
that definitely

06:53.560 --> 06:56.810
we credit with the name
algebra, comes from Arabic

06:56.810 --> 07:00.080
for restoration, and some
people also consider him to be,

07:00.080 --> 07:02.170
if not the father of
algebra, although some people

07:02.170 --> 07:05.370
say he is the father, he is
one of the fathers of algebra

07:05.370 --> 07:08.340
because he really started
to think about algebra

07:08.340 --> 07:11.410
in the abstract sense, devoid
of some specific problems

07:11.410 --> 07:13.440
and a lot of the way
a modern mathematician

07:13.440 --> 07:17.110
would start to think
about the field.
